  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 343 AM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nAn amplified upper pattern is in place across the CONUS this \nmorning. Strong upper ridging is centered over the Upper Midwest, \nacting as somewhat of an Omega Block. This has helped develop two \ncut-off upper lows, one stronger low near the Outer Banks and a \nweaker one over Arizona and New Mexico. The latter low will be the \nfocus for our weather over the next few days, as it steadily lifts \nnortheast through Saturday, transitioning to an open wave in the \nprocess. Within the broad southerly flow ahead of the low, pockets \nof weak isentropic ascent will lead to brief periods of showers and \nweak storms. Tough to pinpoint exact timing and coverage of these, \nbut water vapor imagery supports short range guidance showing one \nwave of showers/storms developing over the next few hours and moving \nnortheast. This would likely be followed by a lull for most of the \nday, with perhaps some lingering very light showers. Persistent \ncloud cover will keep temperatures mostly in the low/mid 70s, though \nsome spots towards central KS could approach 80 if the clouds can \nthin.\n\nAs the main wave approaches tonight, another round of more numerous \nshowers/storms appears likely. Coverage should be highest towards \ncentral KS closer to the better upper forcing. Instability remains \nweak, but similar to yesterday any persistent areas of convection \ncould result in localized 1-2\" rainfall amounts. Expecting \ndecreasing precipitation coverage throughout the morning as the \nupper wave pulls off to the north. This should allow for pockets of \nclearing, with temperatures climbing into the low 80s. Watching the \npotential for a few evening or overnight thunderstorms to develop \nnear a weak frontal boundary. This is somewhat uncertain given \nsubtle height rises, and possible displacement of better instability \nto the west. But with 25-30 kts of effective shear, any storm that \ndoes develop could produce some gusty winds or small hail. \n\nStronger upper ridging builds over the region Sunday into Monday. So \nconfidence is higher in a couple of warmer and drier days. Highs \nshould climb into the low 80s, particularly if winds can turn more \nsoutherly in the wake of Saturday night's weak front.\n\nAttention then turns to a stronger upper low that will move across \nthe southwest CONUS early next week before approaching the Plains \nmid-week. As is typical for such an upper low 5 days out, there is \nplenty of spread in exact track and timing. So confidence in the \ndetails is low. However guidance is very confident in a deep surge \nof tropical moisture ahead of the low, potentially directly tied to \nrecurving Hurricane Polo. ECMWF ensemble mean PWATs are already \napproaching 2\", around record values for this time of year. Coupled \nwith the potentially slow movement of the system, we'll have to \nwatch out for heavy rains and perhaps some flooding mid-week.",
